rner of the room where Itoh Fujiko was sitting. [1221] \"So, I, Dr. Bow Tsung, and... Onizuka will go in,\" Shigemura said, looking at everyone. \"Nagashima will stay at the door. Since there are only women left in the room, if anyone comes out...\"\n[1222] \"What about me?\" Inoue asked cautiously. [1223] \"Inoue-sensei, hmm... what do you think?\" Shigemura asked. [1224] \"If it's not too much trouble, I would like to see Miss Akita's room as well,\" Inoue quickly replied. \"It might be impolite, but I am very interested in her life for the past fifteen years.\" [1225] It seemed that no one had heard Inoue's second half of the sentence. Momiji stood silently next to Isoda Fujiko. Inoue was somewhat surprised that she didn't say she wanted to go as well. Perhaps she felt it was not appropriate. [1226] Yamanaka, Dr. Yamauchi, Onizuka, and Saito entered the yellow door together. [1227] Yamanaka placed his right hand on the aluminum door inside and the door opened quietly. [1228] \"How do you open this door from the inside?\" asked Saito. [1229] \"The same way as outside. Dr. Makoto was supposed to open it\u2026\" answered Yamanaka. [1230] The room was pitch black. Wakamatsu used the flashlight he brought to shine on the wall and found the light switch. [1231] Wakayama walked over and pressed the switch with the end of his pen. The lights came on, and they asked Deborah to keep the aluminum door open, and then entered the room. [1232] The room was large, like a living room. One wall was occupied by bookshelves. Near the entrance door was a black wooden vase with a pattern of totem poles and human faces. Shimizu thought it was a folk art from Africa. There was a small sofa and a glass and aluminum table. This room seemed to have never received any guests. What was surprising was that there were rows of encyclopedias, literary collections, and art history books on the shelves, but no textbooks on information engineering. [1233] The living room had no unusual objects. It